+## TL;DR
+- `openai/whisper`, `SYSTRAN/faster-whisper`, and `ggerganov/whisper.cpp` are the core open source stack for podcast transcription.
+- `pyannote/pyannote-audio` and `snakers4/silero-vad` are strong picks for speaker turns, VAD, and multi-speaker cleanup pipelines.
+- `facebookresearch/demucs`, `Rikorose/DeepFilterNet`, and `resemble-ai/resemble-enhance` cover isolation, denoising, and speech restoration.
+- `coqui-ai/TTS`, `myshell-ai/OpenVoice`, and `yl4579/StyleTTS2` are the most useful voice-generation repos for intros, edits, dubbing, and narration experiments.
+- Purpose-built podcast workflow repos are still a thinner category on GitHub, so this list honestly leans on adjacent open source audio AI projects that podcasters can actually use.
+
+## Why This List
+Open source matters for podcasters because audio workflows are messy, repetitive, and privacy-sensitive. The best AI tools for podcasters 2026 are not just about flashy demos; they need to fit real production pipelines, work on local files, expose code you can audit, and avoid locking your archive into a proprietary web app. This list focuses only on projects with public GitHub repositories so you can self-host, fork, script, or combine them into your own recording and post-production stack.
+
+## Open Source Tools
+
+### Speech-to-Text and Transcription
