+# Contributing
+
+Thanks for improving this awesome list.
+
+## Submission Rules
+
+- Every submission must include a public `github.com` repository URL.
+- No proprietary SaaS products.
+- No tools without a visible open source license.
+- The tool must be relevant to podcasters, even if it is part of a broader audio or speech workflow.
+- Include current GitHub metadata when possible: stars, primary language, license, and last commit date.
+
+## How to Submit
+
+1. Fork the repository.
+2. Add your tool in the most relevant category in `README.md`.
+3. Follow the existing entry format exactly.
+4. If a category is thin, document the gap honestly instead of forcing weak additions.
+5. Open a pull request with links to the repository and any source material you used to verify metadata.
+
+## Entry Format
+
+```md
+#### [Tool Name](https://github.com/username/repo)
+> **Description:** Short summary of what the repo does and why it matters for podcasters.
+
+- **GitHub:** [github.com/username/repo](https://github.com/username/repo)
+- **Stars:** 1234 ⭐
+- **Language:** Python
+- **License:** MIT
+- **Last Commit:** Jan 1, 2026
+- **Category:** tag1, tag2
+- **Best for:** specific use case
+```
+
+## Quality Bar
+
+- Prefer mature and maintained repositories.
+- Prefer tools with README documentation that explains real capabilities.
+- Do not add duplicate repos under multiple names.
+- If a tool stops being public or loses its GitHub repository, it should be removed.
